menu "espOS flow"

    config ESPOS_FLOW_TASK_STACK
        int "Flow loop task stack (bytes)"
        default 6144
        range 2048 32768
        help
            Every node in every graph runs on this one stack: a Poll's read
            function, a Lambda's arithmetic, a Sink's publish, all nested as
            deep as the longest chain. 6 KB carries the built-in nodes and a
            reasonable application with room to spare; raise it if your own
            transform uses a large local buffer, and remember that a chain is
            call depth, not iteration — ten nodes in a row are ten frames.

            Comes from internal RAM like every task stack, which on a board
            with PSRAM is the scarce pool.

    config ESPOS_FLOW_TASK_PRIO
        int "Flow loop task priority"
        default 5
        range 1 24
        help
            Above the idle and housekeeping tasks and below the WiFi/network
            stack (tskIDLE_PRIORITY+18 on IDF), so a busy graph never starves
            the radio and a busy radio only delays the graph. Raise it only if
            a timer's jitter genuinely matters more than the network's.

    config ESPOS_FLOW_MAILBOX_DEPTH
        int "Flow mailbox depth (messages)"
        default 32
        range 4 256
        help
            How many pieces of work other tasks and interrupts may have queued
            for the flow loop at once (8 bytes each, reserved for the life of
            the firmware). Posting never blocks: past this depth a post is
            dropped and counted, and health condition "flowMailbox" is raised
            as a WARNING once.

            Deep enough that a burst of interrupts does not lose data, shallow
            enough that a permanently overloaded loop is visible rather than
            silently latent. If drops appear, the fix is usually a slower
            producer or a faster consumer, not a bigger queue.

    config ESPOS_FLOW_MAX_TIMERS
        int "Timer slots"
        default 32
        range 4 256
        help
            Slots for espos_flow_every()/after() and every Poll and Ticker node
            (~24 bytes each, reserved for the life of the firmware). Exhaustion
            is ESP_ERR_NO_MEM and a loud log line at wiring time, never a
            silently dropped timer.

    config ESPOS_FLOW_MAX_EDGES
        int "Graph edges"
        default 96
        range 8 1024
        help
            The static pool every connect_to() takes an edge from (~12 bytes
            each). One edge per connection, so a chain of five nodes is four
            edges and a producer feeding three consumers is three.

            Exhaustion aborts at wiring time with the node ids involved rather
            than dropping the connection: a graph that silently lost an edge
            would look like a sensor that stopped working months later.

    config ESPOS_FLOW_CHECK_TASK
        bool "Assert that emit() runs on the flow task"
        default y
        help
            The whole no-locks-in-nodes argument rests on one rule: everything
            in a graph runs on the flow task, and anything else comes in
            through a Mailbox. With this on, an emit() from the wrong task
            fails immediately, naming the task, instead of corrupting a node's
            state in a way that surfaces as an impossible value next season.

            Costs one task-handle comparison per emit. Leave it on unless a
            profile says otherwise; the class of bug it catches is the
            expensive one.

    config ESPOS_FLOW_HEALTH
        bool "Report flow conditions to espos_health"
        default y
        help
            Raises "flowMailbox" (WARN) the first time a post is dropped, so a
            loop that cannot keep up shows on the health page and in SignalK
            notifications instead of only in the counters. Turn off for a
            firmware that does not build espos_health.

endmenu
